  "the_plateau_is_necessary_but_not_sufficient": {
    "what_the_extent_plateau_shows": "That the annotation stops pinning the bounding box at 160px of exclusion and the extent reading stops changing.",
    "what_it_does_not_show": "That the box contains only annotation. The extent stays flat while a wider box removes interior subject pixels that were never on the boundary.",
    "what_the_pair_diff_shows_instead": "Exactly zero differing pixels through 260px and thousands from 320px, because two reconstructions of one view differ wherever real geometry sits. The two controls disagree above 260px and the pair diff is right.",
    "consequence": "The pair diff is the load-bearing control. The plateau is corroboration."
  },
  "a_figure_this_corrects": {
    "figure_the_record_carried": 0.0042,
    "measured_at_exclusion_width": 440,
    "corrected_figure": 0.0021,
    "measured_at_certified_width": 290,
    "why_the_old_one_was_too_high": "At 440px the box provably contains subject geometry -- the matched pairs differ by thousands of pixels inside it -- so the movement it produced was the spread responding to removed subject content, not to the annotation. Roughly double the true figure.",
    "how_it_got_past_me": "I took the extent plateau as licence to use the widest box in the sweep, because the extent was flat there. Flat extent means the annotation no longer pins the bounding box; it does not mean the box is empty of subject. The control I needed was the one I already had."
  },
